  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 118 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSurface high pressure centered to our north will provide us with \nanother dry day Friday as it slides slowly east. A mostly sunny sky \nand relatively comfortable temperatures/humidity for late July can \nbe expected Friday, although high temperatures will be a couple \ndegrees warmer than they were Thursday. \n\nModels are struggling with the weekend system, with a somewhat \nuneven but noticeable trend to keep the low pressure system and \nbetter forcing a bit further north. With the poor model consistency, \nthe confidence level in the details is still lacking.  At this time, \nmodels generally show deep moisture increasing Saturday with PWAT \nvalues approaching or exceeding 2 inches across much of the area as \na low pressure system tracks into the Ohio Valley and/or southern \nGreat Lakes Region.  This system pulls a cold front toward us during \nthe day, with it likely moving through our area Saturday night/early \nSunday. This scenario would likely result in an initial round of \nshowers/storms that moves in during the morning with the passage of \na shortwave trough, with this convection possibly elevated and \nlikely sub-severe. A line of convection then would form in the \nafternoon to our west near the cold front and closer to the main \nupper trough axis. The NAM (which has much more CAPE than the GFS) \nis quite vigorous in developing this convection as it crosses Middle \nTN, but by the time the convection reaches our area (well after \nsunset) the NAM shows a significant weakening trend as it moves into \nour area with the decreasing CAPE, the main upper jet support well \nto our north, and downslope low level flow off the eastern \nmountains. While there is some deep layer shear present, the \nnighttime arrival would limit surface-based instability. Ensemble \njoint probability of CAPE > 500 J/kg and effective shear > 30 kt is \nunder 20% in our area Saturday evening into Saturday night. Thus, \nthe severe risk appears still appears to be low at this time. \nFlooding may be more of a concern especially if heavy rain occurs \nwith the morning round, followed by additional heavy rain in the \nsame areas during the evening/overnight round given the abundant \ndeep moisture. \n\nThe ensemble approach of the NBM still has high chances for showers \nand storms Sunday with just a gradual lowering of chances as we move \nthrough the Monday through Thursday time frame. This wet pattern is \nfavored by many of the models which have been leaving behind a weak \nupper low as the main trough exits.  This upper low then meanders \nslowly and eventually fills over the region.  However, it should be \nnoted that the models typically struggle with the location of these \ntypes of weak cutoff lows several days out.  A somewhat drier \nscenario is favored by some models, with the cutoff upper low \nlocated further to the north and/or northeast or the upper trough \ncleanly exiting with upper ridge edging slowly in from the west.  We \nwill have to see which scenario plays out, but for now the forecast \nwill be low confidence but still following the wetter NBM for next \nweek.",
